 ## A headless run by hand
 
-A headless run's workspace runs only the session wrapper: there is no agent screen, no dialog and no input box, so nothing is typed into it and no `answer_prompt` or `stuck_exit` ask opens for it. A `worker_question` is asked and answered as usual; the supervisor sends `answer to ask N: ...` as the next turn. A `stalled` ask (`turn_without_receipt`, `permission_denied`) is answered `wait`, `propose`, `stop` (the supervisor has the session exit; the run ends without a receipt and goes to its recovery job), or with an instruction text, which becomes the next turn. It offers no `intervene`: the turns are read before answering, and an `intervene` that still arrives closes the ask and opens a new one (`skills/dagq-recover/reference/stalled.md`).
+A headless run is a background process that runs only the session wrapper: there is no workspace, no agent screen, no dialog and no input box, so nothing is typed into it and no `answer_prompt` or `stuck_exit` ask opens for it. A `worker_question` is asked and answered as usual; the supervisor sends `answer to ask N: ...` as the next turn. A `stalled` ask (`turn_without_receipt`, `permission_denied`) is answered `wait`, `propose`, `stop` (the supervisor has the session exit; the run ends without a receipt and goes to its recovery job), or with an instruction text, which becomes the next turn. It offers no `intervene`: the turns are read before answering, and an `intervene` that still arrives closes the ask and opens a new one (`skills/dagq-recover/reference/stalled.md`).
 
-With `[headless] wrapper = "background"` in the repository's `dagq.toml`, a headless session runs as a background process with no workspace at all; a runtime planner on the headless route too. `show` and `status` then give the run's `background` (`pid`, `start`, `log`), and `planners` a planner's. Its `[dagq]` line of each turn (the start, the agent's text, its tools, the outcome) goes to that log instead of a terminal: read it with `"$DAGQ" run log RUN` (the run id, or the task id for its latest run; `--lines N` for the last lines) or `"$DAGQ" planner log ID`, and follow a live session with `--follow` until its wrapper ends. The CLI finds the log from the queue, an ended run's and a closed planner's included. A session in a workspace has none (`run screen`). Each turn's raw output stays under `turns/`, and `"$DAGQ" timeline RUN` gives the times.
+A worker's and a runtime planner's session always runs as a background process with no workspace, whatever the repository's `dagq.toml` says. `show` and `status` give the run's `background` (`pid`, `start`, `log`), and `planners` a planner's. Its `[dagq]` line of each turn (the start, the agent's text, its tools, the outcome) goes to that log instead of a terminal: read it with `"$DAGQ" run log RUN` (the run id, or the task id for its latest run; `--lines N` for the last lines) or `"$DAGQ" planner log ID`, and follow a live session with `--follow` until its wrapper ends. The CLI finds the log from the queue, an ended run's and a closed planner's included. An old session in a workspace, from before sessions ran in the background, has none. Each turn's raw output stays under `turns/`, and `"$DAGQ" timeline RUN` gives the times.
